@startuml vinga-barge-in-decision
' The gate an utterance must pass before it may cancel a reply already
' streaming. This is a decision structure, not a flow, which is why it
' gets its own picture: the conversation-turn diagram draws the happy
' path and has nowhere to put branches.
'
' Source: Session._finish_utterance and Session._gate_barge_in, and the
' ADR "replies cancel only on evidence of speech".

title vinga: may this utterance cancel the reply in flight?

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam backgroundColor white
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam ActivityBorderThickness 1
skinparam ArrowColor #555555

start

:An utterance ended while a reply is streaming;
note right
  The session marks activity first, whatever
  follows: an utterance that ended is somebody
  talking, whether or not it earns a reply.
  That is what the idle timeout is specified by.
end note

if (server.barge_in enabled?) then (no)
  :**Drop the utterance**, keep the reply;<<#F4CCCC>>
  note right
    For a board whose echo cancellation leaks its
    own voice back. Mic frames are already filtered
    upstream in _handle_audio, so what reaches here
    is a manual `listen stop` mid-reply.
  end note
  stop
else (yes)
endif

if (who decided the utterance ended?) then (manual `listen stop`)
  :**Cancel the reply** unconditionally;<<#D9EAD3>>
  note right
    The user is holding the button and speaking.
    That is a deliberate act, not an acoustic
    guess, so no gate applies.
    event: barge_in
  end note
  stop
else (the endpointer, acoustically)
endif

partition "The gates, in order" {

  if (classified speech >= server.barge_in_min_speech_ms?\n(500 ms by default)) then (no)
    :**Drop it**, keep the reply;<<#F4CCCC>>
    note right
      A noise blip, not an interjection. Only the
      endpointer can tell the two apart, which is
      why it reports speech_ms at all.
      event: barge_in_suppressed, reason = min_speech
    end note
    stop
  else (yes)
  endif

  if (is the reply still inside ASR?\n(nothing has been spoken yet)) then (yes)
    :Cancel the reply and **prepend its audio**;<<#FFE9C6>>
    :Answer the merged utterance as one;
    note right
      The reply in flight was transcribing the head
      of this same sentence. Merging means one reply
      answers the whole thing rather than two replies
      answering half each.
      event: barge_in_merged
    end note
    stop
  else (no, it is speaking)
  endif

  if (within server.barge_in_refractory_ms of\nthe reply's first audio frame?\n(1000 ms by default)) then (yes)
    :**Drop it**, keep the reply;<<#F4CCCC>>
    note right
      What the microphone hears just after playback
      starts is as likely the onset transient the
      board's echo cancellation let through as it is
      the user.
      event: barge_in_suppressed, reason = refractory
    end note
    stop
  else (no)
  endif

  :**Pause** the outgoing frame pacing;<<#FFE9C6>>
  note right
    A pause, not a stop. Audio ceases within a frame
    either way; what pausing preserves is the option
    of resuming where it left off.
  end note

  :Ask ASR to transcribe the interruption;

  if (did the ASR call fail?) then (yes)
    :Resume the paced stream, keep the reply;<<#F4CCCC>>
    stop
  else (no, it answered)
  endif

  if (is the transcript non-empty?) then (no)
    :**Resume** the paced stream where it stopped;<<#F4CCCC>>
    note right
      The pacing clock is shifted by the pause, so the
      stream picks up rather than bursting to catch up.
      A wrong pause therefore costs one ASR latency,
      not a reply.
      event: barge_in_suppressed, reason = no_transcript
    end note
    stop
  else (yes, the user said words)
  endif

  :**Cancel the reply** and answer the interruption;<<#D9EAD3>>
  note right
    Evidence of user speech, which is the whole rule:
    acoustics alone can at most pause a reply, never
    cancel it.
    event: barge_in (with speech_ms and speaking_ms)
  end note
}

stop

legend right
|<#D9EAD3> Colour |= Outcome |
|<#D9EAD3> | The reply is cancelled, the user is answered |
|<#FFE9C6> | Reversible: paused, or merged into one reply |
|<#F4CCCC> | The utterance is dropped, the reply survives |

Every branch emits a structured event. Those events are what
the two thresholds get tuned from, and they are the decision
track a session capture writes alongside the stereo WAV, where
channel 0 is the microphone as received and channel 1 is what
was paced out to the speaker. Echo leakage is then a
measurement rather than a guess.
endlegend

@enduml
